# FLYEASY - Kubernetes Deployment (Amazon EKS)

This covers deploying the `frontend` and `backend` folders of the FLYEASY
project to an EKS cluster.

## Layout

```
k8s/
├── 00-namespace.yaml         # Namespace: flyeasy
├── 10-ingress.yaml           # ALB Ingress, routes / -> frontend, /api -> backend
├── backend/
│   ├── configmap.yaml
│   ├── secret.yaml           # template only - see warning inside
│   ├── storageclass.yaml     # dynamic EBS provisioning
│   ├── pv.yaml                # static PV example (optional, see notes)
│   ├── pvc.yaml
│   ├── deployment.yaml
│   ├── service.yaml
│   └── hpa.yaml
└── frontend/
    ├── deployment.yaml
    ├── service.yaml
    └── hpa.yaml
```

## Before you apply anything

Everything marked `REPLACE_ME_...` needs a real value:

1. **Container images** - build and push the `frontend` and `backend`
   folders to Amazon ECR (or your registry of choice), then update the
   `image:` field in `frontend/deployment.yaml` and `backend/deployment.yaml`.
2. **Ports** - I assumed backend listens on `8080` and frontend (built
   static assets served by something like nginx) on `80`. Adjust
   `containerPort` / `targetPort` in the Deployments and Services to match
   your actual app.
3. **Health check paths** - I assumed a `/health` endpoint on the backend.
   If your API doesn't have one, either add a lightweight one or change the
   `readinessProbe` / `livenessProbe` paths.
4. **Secrets** - never commit real secret values. Create the Secret
   imperatively (see comment at the top of `backend/secret.yaml`) or use
   AWS Secrets Manager with the External Secrets Operator.
5. **Domain** - set your real domain in `10-ingress.yaml`, or remove the
   `host` field to accept any hostname while testing.

## Cluster prerequisites

These are cluster-level add-ons, not part of this manifest set - install
once per cluster:

- **metrics-server** - required for the HPAs to work
- **aws-ebs-csi-driver** - required for the PVC/PV (persistent storage)
- **aws-load-balancer-controller** - required for the ALB Ingress

```bash
eksctl create addon --cluster <cluster-name> --name metrics-server
eksctl create addon --cluster <cluster-name> --name aws-ebs-csi-driver
# aws-load-balancer-controller is installed via Helm, see AWS docs
```

## Apply order

```bash
kubectl apply -f 00-namespace.yaml
kubectl apply -f backend/configmap.yaml
kubectl apply -f backend/secret.yaml
kubectl apply -f backend/storageclass.yaml
kubectl apply -f backend/pvc.yaml
kubectl apply -f backend/deployment.yaml
kubectl apply -f backend/service.yaml
kubectl apply -f backend/hpa.yaml
kubectl apply -f frontend/deployment.yaml
kubectl apply -f frontend/service.yaml
kubectl apply -f frontend/hpa.yaml
kubectl apply -f 10-ingress.yaml
```

Or simply: `kubectl apply -f 00-namespace.yaml -f backend/ -f frontend/ -f 10-ingress.yaml`

## Verify

```bash
kubectl get all -n flyeasy
kubectl get ingress -n flyeasy      # wait for an ADDRESS (ALB DNS name)
kubectl get hpa -n flyeasy
kubectl get pvc -n flyeasy
```

Point your domain's DNS (or test with the ALB's DNS name directly) once
the Ingress has an address.

## About the PV / PVC

On EKS, the recommended path is **dynamic provisioning**: the
`StorageClass` + `PersistentVolumeClaim` in this repo are enough - Kubernetes
creates the matching EBS volume and PV automatically when the PVC binds.
`backend/pv.yaml` is included as a deliverable and shows the static
alternative (binding to a specific pre-existing EBS volume), but you likely
don't need to apply it unless you're attaching a volume restored from a
snapshot or otherwise created outside the cluster.

## Optional: Helm

If you'd rather manage this as a Helm chart instead of raw YAML (e.g. to
parameterize image tags per environment), let me know and I can convert
this into a chart with `values.yaml` for dev/staging/prod.